   "note": "1.8 Fig 12 second caption (old alt)"
  },
  {
   "old": "<figcaption>A line graph, titled &ldquo;Carbon Family,&rdquo; is shown where the y-axis is labeled &ldquo;Temperature, ( degree sign C )&rdquo; and has values of &ldquo;negative 200&rdquo; to &ldquo;negative 40&rdquo; from bottom to top in increments of 20. The x-axis is labeled &ldquo;Period&rdquo; and has values of &ldquo;0&rdquo; to &ldquo;5&rdquo; in increments of 1. The first point on the graph is labeled &ldquo;C H subscript 4&rdquo; and is at point &ldquo;2, negative 160.&rdquo; The second point on the graph is labeled &ldquo;S i H subscript 4&rdquo; and is at point &ldquo;3, negative 120&rdquo; while the third point on the graph is labeled &ldquo;G e H subscript 4&rdquo; and is at point &ldquo;4, negative 100.&rdquo; The fourth point on the graph is labeled &ldquo;S n H subscript 4&rdquo; and is at point &ldquo;5, negative 60.&rdquo;. (Figure from <em>The Basics of General, Organic, and Biological Chemistry</em> by Ball, Hill and Scott, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0.)</figcaption>",
   "new": "<figcaption>Boiling points of the group 14 hydrides, CH<sub>4</sub> through SnH<sub>4</sub>: boiling point rises as the molecules get larger and dispersion forces get stronger. (Figure from <em>The Basics of General, Organic, and Biological Chemistry</em> by Ball, Hill and Scott, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0.)</figcaption>",
